          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-129447",
          ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+(-1)=2\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{1}=2+1+1=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=4$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $\u27e84,\\frac{1}{2},-1\u27e9.$"
