          ":question/id": "483bef09-2673-515c-b6c8-05e28c9a8828",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-20450",
          ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-5=-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}+5=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{7}{2}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-(5)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\u27f9\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}+5+7=\\frac{25}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{25}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{7}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=5.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$"
        }
