            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186073445,
              ":question/id": "e9aae52c-65f3-5dff-bc6b-a530f8c7acc1",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13009",
              ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=4 \\\\ x+2y &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =4 \\\\ x & =4+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tx+2y &=16 \\\\\n\t(4+y) + 2y &=16\\\\\n\t4+3y &=16\\\\\n\t3y &=12\\\\\n\ty &=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into $x=4+y$, then we can find the value of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tx &= 4 + y \\\\\n\t   &= 4 + (4) \\\\\n\t   &=8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4).$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "5e79a91b-c479-53da-9f06-59c082fa4187",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186073753,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186073446,
              ":question/id": "707504e8-a367-5dc2-a663-371a9dcf7224",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13010",
              ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2u+9v-6 &=0 \\\\ 2u+6v-8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n\n$$\n\\qquad\\:\\:\\: \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}2u+9v-6} &{\\color{red}{}=0} && {\\color{red}(1)} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2u+6v-8} &{\\color{blue}{}=0} && {\\color{blue}(2)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\\\n3v &= -2 \\\\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\\\n\tu &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "b4352698-7af7-5d74-9c3a-0f951c3e6078",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"
          }
